# Gowin GW2A, as much of it as Reticle can state confidently. One part:
# the GW2A-LV18PG256C8/I7 of a Sipeed Tang Primer 20K.
#
# NOTHING BUILT FROM THIS FILE HAS BEEN LOADED INTO A PART. The board is
# on the machine and `reticle program --device "FactoryAIOT Pro" --expect
# 0000081b --probe` reads its JTAG IDCODE over its FT2232D adapter, and
# that is the whole of what has touched the hardware. No design has been
# placed on it, no bitstream has been sent to it, and the JTAG
# configuration sequence that would send one is not written. Compare
# `xc7.dev`, where one design of one lookup table and three pins has run
# on a Basys 3; there is no equivalent sentence here and this file must
# not be read as if there were.
#
# What the test suite does check is that the netlist a design synthesises
# to holds only primitives this file declares, wired to pins they have,
# and — separately, in `tests/fpga_gowin.rs` — that the fabric
# `fpga::apicula` loads out of Project Apicula's own chip database has the
# resources the counts below claim. Several of those counts are
# *measured from that database* rather than transcribed, which is said
# where it is true.
#
# Provenance and confidence, stated per group because it differs:
#
#   * THE RESOURCE COUNTS ARE MEASURED, NOT RECALLED. 20736 LUT4, 15552
#     flip-flops, 15552 ALUs, 648 distributed RAMs, 384 IO buffers, 46
#     block RAMs, 12 DSP blocks and 4 PLLs are what you get by counting
#     the bels of every tile of `GW2A-18.msgpack.xz`'s 55 x 56 grid, and
#     `tests/fpga_gowin.rs::the_fabric_measures_itself_and_the_documented_ones`
#     re-counts them from the database so they cannot drift. They agree
#     with Gowin's published figures for a GW2A-18 (20736 LUT4, 15552
#     registers, 828 kbit of block RAM as 46 x 18 kbit, 48 18x18
#     multipliers in 12 blocks, 4 rPLLs). HIGH confidence.
#   * LUT4: four inputs I0..I3, output F, a 16-bit INIT, and the model is
#     `F = INIT[{I3,I2,I1,I0}]` with I0 the least significant bit of the
#     index. HIGH confidence for the pins: the chip database's own bel
#     portmap for every one of a tile's eight LUTs is
#     `{'F': 'F<n>', 'I0': 'A<n>', 'I1': 'B<n>', 'I2': 'C<n>', 'I3':
#     'D<n>'}`, read from the file rather than from a document. That is
#     also Reticle's own LUT convention, so a mapped truth table is
#     written out unchanged and the `i=` list below is in pin order.
#     `lut_size` is 4, which is the same technology-mapping path the
#     iCE40 and the ECP5 already use.
#     LUT1..LUT3 are declared too, with their own INIT widths, because
#     `gowin_pack` accepts them (it has a `get_LUT1_fuses` and so on) and
#     a netlist check should know them; Reticle itself always emits LUT4,
#     since [`super::techcells`] widens a narrower table by repeating it.
#     A NOTE ON THE POLARITY OF THE BITS, which is not visible here and
#     matters: on this family a configuration fuse is set when an INIT
#     bit is ZERO. That lives in `fpga::apicula`, which emits
#     `ConfigEntry::ParamZero`, and it is read off
#     `gowin_pack.py::get_LUT4_fuses` (`if lutbit == '0': bits.update(
#     lutmap[bitnum])`). Getting it backwards inverts every lookup table
#     in a design.
#   * MUX2_LUT5, MUX2_LUT6, MUX2_LUT7 and MUX2_LUT8 are declared `other`.
#     They are the wide multiplexers that combine two, four, eight or
#     sixteen LUT4s, and `gowin_pack` sets no fuses for them at all
#     (`get_MUX2_LUT5_fuses` returns an empty list) because the
#     multiplexer is hard-wired between the LUTs. Reticle has no wide-mux
#     mapping, so it never instantiates one.
#   * FLIP-FLOPS. Twenty primitives: DFF, DFFE, DFFR, DFFRE, DFFS,
#     DFFSE, DFFC, DFFCE, DFFP, DFFPE and a DFFN* twin of each that
#     captures on the FALLING clock edge. HIGH confidence in the NAMES —
#     that list is exactly the set of `get_DFF*_fuses` methods
#     `apycula/gowin_pack.py` implements, which is the set of cell types
#     the reference packer accepts. MEDIUM-HIGH confidence in the PORT
#     NAMES (D, CLK, Q, CE, and SET / RESET / PRESET / CLEAR): those are
#     Gowin's *Primitives User Guide* SUG283, which is documentation
#     rather than a source this machine checked. The four set/reset kinds
#     map as RESET synchronous-reset, SET synchronous-set, CLEAR
#     asynchronous-reset, PRESET asynchronous-set, which is also what the
#     database's `REG0_REGSET` attribute (values `RESET` and `SET`) and
#     its `SRMODE` attribute (values `ASYNC` and `LSR_OVER_CE`) say
#     between them.
#     THREE CONSEQUENCES WORTH STATING PLAINLY:
#       - A flip-flop with no clock enable is a *different primitive*
#         from one with an enable, not the same one with CE tied high,
#         so each combination is its own line and there are twenty.
#       - There is no combination of a synchronous and an asynchronous
#         set/reset on one Gowin flip-flop, and none with both a set and
#         a reset. One kind per flop.
#       - Every control pin is active high. An active-low reset costs one
#         LUT4 inverter per net, which is what flip-flop mapping inserts
#         and reports. (The fabric *can* invert: the database's
#         `LSR_MUX_LSR` attribute takes the value `INV`. Using it would
#         need the attribute names the database does not carry — see
#         `docs/fpga-gowin.md` — so no `active_low` line is declared.)
#     `shared_reset` IS SET, AND IT IS MEASURED. A logic tile holds six
#     flip-flops in three pairs, and the database's own portmaps say the
#     pairs share their control: DFF0 and DFF1 are both
#     `{'CLK': 'CLK0', 'LSR': 'LSR0', 'CE': 'CE0'}`, DFF2 and DFF3 both
#     `CLK1/LSR1/CE1`, DFF4 and DFF5 both `CLK2/LSR2/CE2`. So two flops
#     with different control sets cannot be packed together. HIGH
#     confidence; this was read out of the file.
#     `init` is deliberately absent from the `ff` line, and so is
#     `param INIT=`: the power-up value follows from which primitive is
#     chosen (DFFR and DFFC to zero, DFFS and DFFP to one) and whether
#     the Gowin library also accepts an `INIT` parameter on these
#     primitives was not verified here, so none is written.
#     A GAP THAT MATTERS FOR ROUTING, NOT FOR THIS FILE: a Gowin
#     flip-flop's D input comes from the LUT beside it *inside* the
#     slice and has no tile wire of its own, exactly as a 7-series
#     `AFF`'s does not. The database's flip-flop portmap has no `D` at
#     all. Until packing can place a LUT and a flip-flop on one slice,
#     nothing sequential will route on the fabric `fpga::apicula` builds.
#   * NO CARRY CHAIN IS DECLARED, and ALU is declared `other`. This is a
#     choice with a reason, and it is the same shape of reason `xc7.dev`
#     gives for leaving out the DSP48E1.
#     Gowin's carry element is the ALU, and the database gives its pins:
#     `{'COUT', 'CIN', 'SUM': 'F<n>', 'I0', 'I1', 'I3'}` — note that it
#     has no I2, which is tied inside — and twelve modes, keyed `0`, `1`,
#     `2`, `3`, `4`, `6`, `7`, `8`, `9`, `C2L`, `ONE2C` and `hadder`.
#     Apicula's `doc/alu.md` names them: 0 add, 1 sub, 2 addsub, 3 ne, 4
#     ge, 5 le (which shares 4's pattern, hence its absence), 6 cup, 7
#     cdn, 8 cupcdn, 9 mul.
#     In ADD mode the ALU computes `SUM = I0 ^ I1 ^ CIN` and
#     `COUT = MAJ(I0, I1, CIN)` — a full adder taking the two OPERANDS.
#     Reticle's `.dev` model has two carry shapes and the ALU is neither:
#     the one-bit shape (`ci i0 i1 co`, the iCE40's SB_CARRY) computes
#     only the carry and expects a *separate* LUT to compute the sum,
#     and the wide shape (`ci cyinit p di s co`, the 7-series CARRY4)
#     takes a PROPAGATE `p = a ^ b` that a separate LUT computes. The
#     Gowin ALU is the slice's LUT4 reconfigured, so there is no
#     separate LUT to put either in — the sum comes out of the same
#     element. Declaring the ALU under either shape would produce a
#     netlist with a LUT4 and an ALU competing for one slice, which the
#     placer cannot satisfy and which no structural check would notice.
#     So adders stay in LUT4s: correct, and large. Teaching the model a
#     "carry element that also produces the sum" is the fix, and it is
#     not done here.
#   * IO BUFFERS. IBUF (pad `I`, fabric `O`) and OBUF (fabric `I`, pad
#     `O`) are declared and mapped. HIGH confidence: Gowin's buffer
#     convention is that `I` is the buffer's input and `O` its output,
#     and it is confirmed by Project Apicula's own example designs, which
#     are built for this very board — `examples/blinky-tbuf.v`
#     instantiates `TBUF(.I, .O, .OEN)`, `examples/emcu-blinky.v`
#     instantiates `IOBUF(.O, .IO, .I, .OEN)` and `examples/elvds.v`
#     instantiates `ELVDS_TBUF(.I, .O, .OB, .OEN)`.
#     TBUF AND IOBUF ARE DECLARED `other` AND NEVER INSTANTIATED, for a
#     specific reason: their output enable is `OEN` and it is ACTIVE LOW
#     — every one of those examples writes `.OEN(~key)` or
#     `.OEN(~gpio_oen[0])`. Reticle's `oe` role is active high and the
#     `.dev` model has no way to say otherwise, so declaring `oe=OEN`
#     would give every tristate design an inverted enable: a bus that
#     drives when it should listen. A tristate port is therefore
#     reported as unbuildable rather than built backwards. Adding an
#     `active_low` marker to the `io` line is the fix.
#     There is no `pad` wire in the fabric either way: a package ball is
#     not a wire a router can reach, which is what `fpga::xray` says
#     about the 7-series IO too.
#     THE DIFFERENTIAL BUFFERS — TLVDS_IBUF, TLVDS_OBUF, TLVDS_TBUF,
#     TLVDS_IOBUF and the ELVDS_* four the database lists in its
#     `diff_io_types` field — are declared `other`. Reticle's
#     constraints model names one pin per port and cannot state a pin
#     *pair*, so it cannot check that `tmds_d_p[0]` and `tmds_d_n[0]` are
#     the two halves of one differential pad. The Tang Primer 20K dock's
#     HDMI connector is exactly this, and it is out of reach until the
#     model grows a pair.
#   * NO CONFIGURATION BITS FOR ANY OF THEM. This is the largest gap and
#     it belongs in this file's account even though it is not a property
#     of this file. A Gowin bel's configuration is a set of attribute
#     values (`IO_TYPE=LVCMOS33`, `PULLMODE=NONE`, `REGMODE=FF`), and
#     the chip database encodes those as small integer *codes* whose
#     NAMES are not in the file — they are Python dictionaries in
#     `apycula/attrids.py`. So `fpga::apicula` configures no bel but the
#     LUT4, whose bits are named by position instead. A bitstream from
#     this flow would route a signal to a pad and leave the pad
#     unconfigured. See `docs/fpga-gowin.md`.
#   * IO STANDARDS. LVCMOS33, LVCMOS25, LVCMOS18, LVCMOS15, LVCMOS12 and
#     LVTTL33 are listed. HIGH confidence for the NAMES: they are values
#     of the database's own `IO_TYPE` attribute
#     (`attrids.iob_attrvals`), and LVCMOS33 is what Apicula's
#     `examples/primer20k.cst` uses for every ordinary pin of this board.
#     HIGH confidence that the slew rates are SLOW and FAST, which are
#     the only two values of the `SLEWRATE` attribute. MEDIUM confidence
#     for the DRIVE LISTS per standard: the attribute takes many numeric
#     values and which are legal with which standard was not read off
#     anything here. Since this flow writes no IO bits at all, a wrong
#     drive list can only accept or refuse a constraint, never
#     mis-configure a pad.
#     THE DIFFERENTIAL STANDARDS (LVDS25, LVDS25E and the rest) ARE NOT
#     LISTED, for the pin-pair reason above.
#   * IO BANKS. Eight, `bank0` .. `bank7`, and which ball is in which is
#     MEASURED: the database's `pin_bank` field maps every `IOLOC` to a
#     bank number and the `pin` lines below carry it. HIGH confidence.
#     THE `vccio` LISTS ARE THE PART'S OPTIONS AND NOT THE BOARD'S
#     WIRING. Every bank is listed as accepting 3.3, 2.5, 1.8, 1.5 and
#     1.2 volts because the part does. What a Tang Primer 20K dock
#     actually ties each bank's VCCIO to is not known here, and Reticle
#     has no way to find out, so a constraint asking for LVCMOS18 on a
#     bank the board wired to 3.3 V will be accepted. DO NOT READ THESE
#     LISTS AS BOARD TRUTH. (`xc7.dev` does the opposite — one bank at
#     3.3 V, because a Basys 3's schematic says so — and the difference
#     is that a schematic was available there.)
#   * PIN LIST: COMPLETE FOR THE PACKAGE'S IO BALLS, AND MEASURED. All
#     207 lines below are the database's own `pinout['GW2A-18']
#     ['PBGA256']` table, generated from it rather than typed: the ball
#     name, the bank `pin_bank` puts it in, and in a trailing comment the
#     special functions the table records for it. The package has 256
#     balls and the other 49 are power and ground, which the table does
#     not carry and this file does not list, so the list says `pins`
#     rather than `pins partial`. HIGH confidence.
#     A ball is marked `clock` when the database gives it a `GCLKT_n` or
#     `GCLKC_n` function — sixteen of them, the true and complement
#     halves of eight differential global clock inputs — and `config`
#     when it gives it a JTAG, mode, or configuration-flash function.
#     Those twenty-one `config` balls can usually be used as ordinary IO
#     and this file does not forbid it; it marks them so a constraint
#     that lands on `TCK` is visible.
#     WHAT THIS FILE DOES NOT SAY IS WHICH BOARD NET EACH BALL REACHES.
#     `xc7.dev` lists Digilent's published board file and can therefore
#     say "V17 is slide switch 0"; there is no equivalent line here,
#     because nothing on this machine holds a Tang Primer 20K schematic.
#     What *is* available, and is used by `tests/fpga_gowin.rs`, is
#     Project Apicula's own `examples/primer20k.cst`, the constraint file
#     its `make primer20k` target builds every one of its example designs
#     with: it names C13, A13, N16, N14, L14 and L16 as the dock's six
#     user LEDs, T3 as a button, H11 as the clock, and A15/D14 as the
#     UART pair. Those are cited in the test rather than written into
#     this file, because a board file is not a device description.
#   * GLOBAL CLOCKS: eight, and NO CLOCK BUFFER PRIMITIVE IS DECLARED.
#     `global_buffers 8` is the eight differential global clock inputs
#     the package's own pinout names (`GCLKT_0` .. `GCLKT_7` with their
#     `GCLKC_n` complements) — measured, HIGH confidence.
#     Unlike the iCE40's SB_GB or the ECP5's DCCA there is no `gb` bel,
#     and that is not an omission: on this family a clock enters the
#     global network *by being routed onto it*, not by instantiating a
#     buffer. The database says so — the 14 748 entries of its `nodes`
#     table, 6306 of whose members are typed `GLOBAL_CLK`, are the
#     network — and a GW2A-18 has no `BUFG` bel anywhere in its grid
#     (`gowin_pack` has a `get_BUFG_fuses`, but for the GW5 series).
#     The things that *do* sit on the clock network — DQCE, DCS, DHCEN,
#     CLKDIV, CLKDIV2 — are declared `other` below so a netlist check
#     knows them.
#   * PLL: one line, rPLL, and IT IS DELIBERATELY NOT CONFIGURABLE.
#     The ports and parameters are HIGH confidence, read off Apicula's
#     own `examples/clock-rPLL.v`: CLKIN, CLKFB, CLKOUT, CLKOUTP,
#     CLKOUTD, CLKOUTD3, LOCK, RESET, RESET_P, and the six-bit IDSEL,
#     FBDSEL and ODSEL dynamic-select buses, with `CLKFB_SEL="internal"`
#     and CLKFB tied low for the ordinary internal-feedback case. The
#     frequency limits are HIGH confidence for a different reason: they
#     are `apycula/gowin_pll.py`'s own table for `GW2A-18 C8/I7`, which
#     cites Gowin's DS117E / DS861E / DS226E — phase detector 3 to 500
#     MHz, VCO 500 to 1250 MHz, output 3.90625 to 625 MHz.
#     The dividers are `IDIV_SEL + 1` on the reference and
#     `FBDIV_SEL + 1` on the feedback, both fields 0..63, and the
#     feedback is taken from the OUTPUT and not the VCO:
#     `CLKOUT = FCLKIN * (FBDIV_SEL + 1) / (IDIV_SEL + 1)` and
#     `VCO = CLKOUT * ODIV_SEL`, which is `feedback out` in this
#     format's terms. All of that is written below.
#     KNOWN GAP, AND THE REASON THIS PLL CANNOT BE USED: the output
#     divider `ODIV_SEL` is NOT a contiguous range. Its legal values are
#     2, 4, 8, 16, 32, 48, 64, 80, 96, 112 and 128, and `PllDivider`
#     describes a divider as a minimum, a maximum and an offset, so
#     declaring `divide out ODIV_SEL 2 128` would let `fpga::pll`'s
#     search choose ODIV_SEL = 3 and write a PLL that does not run at
#     the frequency it says. Rather than risk that, no `divide out`
#     clause is written, which makes `PllShape::is_configurable` false,
#     which makes a design asking for a PLL fail with a message naming
#     the missing divider. Teaching `PllDivider` an explicit value list
#     is the fix; everything else the solver needs is already here.
#   * NO DSP. The part has twelve DSP blocks — 48 MULT18X18, and the
#     database names every sub-bel of them (MULT9X9, MULT18X18,
#     MULT36X36, PADD9, PADD18, ALU54D, MULTALU18X18, MULTALU36X18,
#     MULTADDALU18X18) — and this file declares none of them as a `dsp`.
#     The reason is `xc7.dev`'s: a Gowin multiplier does not multiply
#     unless ASEL, BSEL, ASIGN, BSIGN and the mode parameters are driven
#     with the right constants, and the `.dev` `dsp` line has no way to
#     state a tied input. Multiplies therefore go to LUT4s. They are
#     declared `other` so a netlist check knows them.
#   * NO BLOCK RAM. The part has 46 of them, 828 kbit, and the database
#     gives the BSRAM bel's whole portmap (DO0..DO35, AD0..AD13,
#     DI0..DI35, CLK, OCE, CE, RESET, WRE, BLKSEL0..2) and the four
#     primitive families `gowin_pack` accepts (SP single-port, SDP
#     semi-dual-port, DP dual-port, ROM). Two things stop it being
#     declared here, and the first is enough on its own:
#       - A block is only selected when its `BLKSEL[2:0]` input matches
#         its `BLK_SEL` parameter, and the `.dev` `bram` model has no way
#         to tie an input to a constant. A block RAM whose BLKSEL is left
#         floating never responds, which is a memory that silently reads
#         zero.
#       - Its initial contents are 64 parameters `INIT_RAM_00` ..
#         `INIT_RAM_3F`, and they do NOT live in the die bitmap the rest
#         of the configuration lives in: `gowin_pack` writes them into a
#         separate region addressed through a `logicinfo['BSRAM_INIT']`
#         table. `fpga::gowin` does not write that region, so a ROM built
#         from this flow would come up blank.
#     Memories therefore go to distributed logic. The primitives are
#     declared `other`.
#   * NO DISTRIBUTED RAM EITHER, and the reason is narrower. The part has
#     648 of them (the `RAM16` bel of every fourth logic column, 16 words
#     of 4 bits, whose database portmap is `{'DI', 'CLK', 'WRE', 'WAD',
#     'RAD', 'DO'}`), and the primitive is RAM16SDP4. The `.dev` `lutram`
#     line names a wide port as a LIST OF INDIVIDUAL PINS
#     (`waddr=WAD0,WAD1,WAD2,WAD3`), which is right for the ECP5's
#     TRELLIS_DPR16X4 and the 7 series' RAM64X1D because those primitives
#     really do have one port per bit; RAM16SDP4's DI, WAD, RAD and DO
#     are Verilog VECTORS, so those pin names do not exist and an
#     instance would not elaborate. Declared `other`.
#   * NO DDR OR SERDES. ODDR, ODDRC, IDDR, IDDRC, OSER4/8/10,
#     IDES4/8/10, OVIDEO and IVIDEO all exist on the part and are
#     declared `other`. A double-data-rate port is reported as
#     unbuildable and buffered as an ordinary one. Their configuration is
#     an `IOLOGIC` attribute set, which is the same missing-names problem
#     as the IO buffer's.
#   * NO TILE GRID CHECK BEYOND THE BOUNDING BOX. `grid 56 55` is the
#     database's own grid, measured. It is a bounding box: a region
#     constraint is checked against it and nothing finer.
#   * `idcode 0x0000081b` is the value the `.fs` header's `0x06` command
#     carries in `GW2A-18.msgpack.xz`, and it is the value
#     `reticle program --probe` reads back from the board over JTAG.
#     HIGH confidence — it was read from both.
#     IT DOES NOT IDENTIFY THE DIE. A GW2A-18, a GW2A-18C and a
#     GW2AR-18C all answer `0x0000081b`, and Apicula ships separate
#     `GW2A-18` and `GW2A-18C` databases with identical grids, identical
#     headers and different block-RAM errata. So the IDCODE check stops a
#     GW1N bitstream reaching a GW2A and nothing finer; which database to
#     use has to be said, and this file says `GW2A-18` because that is
#     what Apicula's own `examples/Makefile` uses for a Tang Primer 20K
#     (`gowin_pack -c -d GW2A-18`, `nextpnr --device
#     GW2A-LV18PG256C8/I7`).
#   * THE INSTRUCTION REGISTER IS EIGHT BITS, where a 7 series' is six.
#     That is not recorded in this file — the `.dev` format has nowhere
#     to put it — and it is why `reticle program` reads this part's
#     IDCODE with `jtag::idcode_after_reset`, which needs no instruction
#     at all. Writing a configuration sequence for this part is phase
#     two and will need it.

device gw2a-18-pg256
  family gowin
  package PBGA256
  speed 8
  idcode 0x0000081b
  lut_size 4
  ff enable sync_reset async_reset shared_reset
  bel LUT4 lut count 20736 port i=I0,I1,I2,I3 o=F param INIT=16'h0000
  bel LUT3 lut port i=I0,I1,I2 o=F param INIT=8'h00
  bel LUT2 lut port i=I0,I1 o=F param INIT=4'h0
  bel LUT1 lut port i=I0 o=F param INIT=2'h0
  bel DFF ff count 15552 mode posedge port clk=CLK d=D q=Q
  bel DFFE ff mode posedge,enable port clk=CLK d=D q=Q en=CE
  bel DFFR ff mode posedge,sync_reset port clk=CLK d=D q=Q rst=RESET
  bel DFFRE ff mode posedge,enable,sync_reset port clk=CLK d=D q=Q en=CE rst=RESET
  bel DFFS ff mode posedge,sync_set port clk=CLK d=D q=Q rst=SET
  bel DFFSE ff mode posedge,enable,sync_set port clk=CLK d=D q=Q en=CE rst=SET
  bel DFFC ff mode posedge,async_reset port clk=CLK d=D q=Q rst=CLEAR
  bel DFFCE ff mode posedge,enable,async_reset port clk=CLK d=D q=Q en=CE rst=CLEAR
  bel DFFP ff mode posedge,async_set port clk=CLK d=D q=Q rst=PRESET
  bel DFFPE ff mode posedge,enable,async_set port clk=CLK d=D q=Q en=CE rst=PRESET
  bel DFFN ff mode negedge port clk=CLK d=D q=Q
  bel DFFNE ff mode negedge,enable port clk=CLK d=D q=Q en=CE
  bel DFFNR ff mode negedge,sync_reset port clk=CLK d=D q=Q rst=RESET
  bel DFFNRE ff mode negedge,enable,sync_reset port clk=CLK d=D q=Q en=CE rst=RESET
  bel DFFNS ff mode negedge,sync_set port clk=CLK d=D q=Q rst=SET
  bel DFFNSE ff mode negedge,enable,sync_set port clk=CLK d=D q=Q en=CE rst=SET
  bel DFFNC ff mode negedge,async_reset port clk=CLK d=D q=Q rst=CLEAR
  bel DFFNCE ff mode negedge,enable,async_reset port clk=CLK d=D q=Q en=CE rst=CLEAR
  bel DFFNP ff mode negedge,async_set port clk=CLK d=D q=Q rst=PRESET
  bel DFFNPE ff mode negedge,enable,async_set port clk=CLK d=D q=Q en=CE rst=PRESET
  bel IBUF io for in port pad=I din=O
  bel OBUF io for out port pad=O dout=I
  bel TBUF other port pad=O dout=I oe=OEN
  bel IOBUF other port pad=IO din=O dout=I oe=OEN
  bel ALU other port ci=CIN co=COUT i0=I0 i1=I1
  bel MUX2_LUT5 other
  bel MUX2_LUT6 other
  bel MUX2_LUT7 other
  bel MUX2_LUT8 other
  bel RAM16SDP4 other
  bel SP other
  bel SDP other
  bel DP other
  bel ROM other
  bel MULT9X9 other
  bel MULT18X18 other
  bel MULT36X36 other
